    "stac_version": "1.1.0",
    "description": "The Copernicus Sentinel-5 Precursor (Sentinel-5P or S5P) satellite mission is one of the European Space Agency's (ESA) new mission family - Sentinels, and it is a joint initiative between the Kingdom of the Netherlands and the ESA. The sole payload on Sentinel-5P is the TROPOspheric Monitoring Instrument (TROPOMI), which is a nadir-viewing 108 degree Field-of-View push-broom grating hyperspectral spectrometer, covering the wavelength of ultraviolet-visible (UV-VIS, 270nm to 495nm), near infrared (NIR, 675nm to 775nm), and shortwave infrared (SWIR, 2305nm-2385nm). Sentinel-5P is the first of the Atmospheric Composition Sentinels and provides measurements of ozone, NO2, SO2, CH4, CO, formaldehyde, aerosols and clouds at high spatial, temporal and spectral resolutions. The spatial resolution for ozone profiles is 30 km along-track and 30 km across-track at nadir with a vertical resolution of 6 km. S5P is in a sun-synchronous orbit with an ascending node of ~13:30 which provides daily global coverage of the sun-lit Earth. Data are archived in enhanced netCDF-4 format. Beginning August 6th in 2019, S5P implemented along-track high spatial resolution (~5.5km at nadir). For data prior to August 6th of 2019 use the standard resolution version of this product.\n\nThis product contains ozone profile with a vertical resolution of 6 km and a horizontal resolution of 30x30km. General algorithm updates for Version 3 include: fixing cloud pressure coregistration, destriping of O3 profile, retrieval quality for high sza/sfc albedo scenes around Antarctica, SNR ratio floor increase, coregistration issue; updates of apriori data, high cloud fraction sensitivity; cloud pressure coregistration has been improved; using different correlation lengths in troposphere and stratosphere; updated a priori data \u2013 the Merra-2 climatology is now used for the a priori; selective Signal-To-Noise ratio ceiling increase (radiance); adjust an a-priori error for SO2; excluding the Mg line at 279\u2013281nm.",
